  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Afghanistan - Floods</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="3">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">140,000 people</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Floods in Afghanistan. Federation-Wide Emergency Appeal
On 10 May 2024, flash floods swept across multiple provinces in the northeast region of Afghanistan, leaving more than 250,000 people homeless.. These floods followed earlier flooding from 16 to 20 April 2024, which had affected 24 provinces.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Federation-Wide Emergency Appeal aims to address the multisectoral needs of the people most affected by the floods in 14 provinces with a budget of 7,5 million CHF (Federation-Wide). Specifically, the operation will contribute to meeting urgent basic needs, including food, shelter, livelihoods, health, WASH, and essential household items through in-kind and cash assistance.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The provinces most impacted by the flash floods were Badakhshan, Baghlan, and Takhar</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The widespread floods across much of Afghanistan, affecting 33 out of the country&apos;s 34 provinces, highlight the massive scale of this disaster and the substantial humanitarian needs that have emerged. This situation occurs against the backdrop of ongoing crises across Afghanistan, following the compounding impacts of multiple shocks, including severe drought, multiple instances of flooding and earthquakes, public health emergencies, and economic hardship.</narrative>
  </description>
